From 17fc9bac485aeba45758d1a41ff6286f0e8c90b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 15:03:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 19/91] fix(debug): measure jank against the session's own
 frame time

A reading off the phone showed `p50 33.3 / p90 33.5 / p99 33.5 / jank 19.2 %`:
the frames were perfectly even, and 19 % of them still counted as jank,
because FRAME_JANK_MS is a hardcoded "two missed 60 Hz vsyncs" = 33.4 ms while
this panel runs at 90 Hz and every third-vsync frame lands on 33.3-33.5 ms. A
number that fires on even frames cannot be trusted, and this one was being read
as evidence of stutter.

Jank is now `delta > max(FRAME_JANK_MS, median * 1.5)`: deviation from what the
session actually achieves, with the old constant kept as a floor so a healthy
60 fps session still flags a two-vsync frame. Low frame rate stays fps's job.

Also stops splitting a word's glow into one motion span per character when the
reveal mode ignores per-character timing. `wordGlowVariants.active` returns
before the sweep branch for `instant` and `fast`, so those spans animated in
lockstep and drew the same pixels as a single span - while the line change they
are mounted on is what the HUD attributes its remaining 83 ms stall to.
